1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,427
The Territory tab had been open for sixteen months
and he had not spent a single Point

2
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:18,550
in it, because for sixteen months he could not
afford the cheapest line on it.

3
00:00:19,699 --> 00:00:27,515
Territory. Industrial district, four hundred acres:
one hundred and eighty thousand. Small

4
00:00:27,535 --> 00:00:35,264
town, population six thousand: four hundred and
twenty thousand. Company city, population

5
00:00:35,284 --> 00:00:43,361
one hundred thousand, with full civic apparatus: one
and a half million. A Territory purchase

6
00:00:43,381 --> 00:00:47,278
creates citizens. Citizens are
not personnel.

7
00:00:47,868 --> 00:00:53,170
One and a half million Points. In April he had
earned one million four hundred and ten

8
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:57,378
thousand in a single quarter, and the third
quarter had been better.

9
00:00:57,899 --> 00:01:00,689
Define "citizens are not personnel".

10
00:01:01,088 --> 00:01:08,526
Personnel work for you. Citizens live under you. They
have opinions, elections, newspapers,

11
00:01:08,546 --> 00:01:15,737
grievances and children. They can dislike you. They
cannot easily leave, because leaving

12
00:01:15,757 --> 00:01:19,507
is expensive and people are not
made of money.

13
00:01:19,906 --> 00:01:21,837
And what do they generate?

14
00:01:22,236 --> 00:01:29,287
Devotion income, continuously, in proportion to how
they feel about you. Or its opposite.

15
00:01:29,688 --> 00:01:35,802
The narrator notes that this is the single most
important sentence anybody says in this

16
00:01:35,822 --> 00:01:40,598
chapter, and that Albert Wesker heard it
exactly as it was intended.

17
00:01:41,117 --> 00:01:42,207
Its opposite.

18
00:01:42,608 --> 00:01:50,138
A hundred thousand people who resent you would cost you
Points every week that they continued

19
00:01:50,158 --> 00:01:55,984
to. I do not recommend acquiring a city you
do not intend to be good to.

20
00:01:56,384 --> 00:01:58,954
Noted. Where would you put it?

21
00:01:59,353 --> 00:02:05,223
He already knew. He had chosen the valley in June,
from the air, on the way back from

22
00:02:05,243 --> 00:02:11,527
Arklay: a wide glacial trough on the west side of the
ridge, eleven miles below the estate,

23
00:02:11,547 --> 00:02:17,417
with a river through it, a disused rail line along
the river, and four thousand acres

24
00:02:17,437 --> 00:02:22,543
of second-growth timber that the state had
been trying to sell since 2011.

25
00:02:23,063 --> 00:02:29,747
There. Below the estate. The rail spur runs down
the valley to the main line at Kane;

26
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:33,454
the road is nineteen miles from
the interstate.

27
00:02:33,854 --> 00:02:37,158
That valley is within a state
forest boundary.

28
00:02:37,558 --> 00:02:43,248
It has been a chartered municipality
since 1953, I expect.

29
00:02:43,649 --> 00:02:44,813
(flat) It has.

30
00:02:45,213 --> 00:02:50,882
On Friday night there was a valley. Trees, a river,
a rail line with saplings growing

31
00:02:50,902 --> 00:02:53,893
through the ballast, and the
sound of an owl.

32
00:02:54,294 --> 00:02:56,634
On Saturday morning there was a city.

33
00:02:57,033 --> 00:03:03,193
A hundred thousand people. Nine and a half thousand
acres. A downtown of eighteen blocks

34
00:03:03,213 --> 00:03:09,654
with a clock on the courthouse and a department store
that had been there since the sixties.

35
00:03:09,674 --> 00:03:16,044
A hospital with four hundred beds. A police department
with two hundred and eighty officers

36
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:22,224
and a chief who had come up through traffic. Four
high schools, one of which had won the

37
00:03:22,244 --> 00:03:28,263
state championship in 1998 and had a mural about it.
A tram line — two lines, in fact,

38
00:03:28,283 --> 00:03:34,653
a red and a green, with rolling stock from 2014. A
stadium that seated thirty-one thousand.

39
00:03:35,054 --> 00:03:40,412
A river with three bridges over it. A cemetery
with twenty-four thousand graves.

40
00:03:40,812 --> 00:03:47,126
The narrator wants to be precise about that cemetery,
because it is the detail that people

41
00:03:47,146 --> 00:03:53,248
cannot get past, and it is the detail that Wesker
himself went and looked at, alone, on

42
00:03:53,268 --> 00:03:54,022
the Sunday.

43
00:03:54,423 --> 00:04:00,520
Twenty-four thousand headstones. Names, dates,
epitaphs. A section for children. Flowers

44
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:06,707
on some of them, dead flowers on others, weeds on the
oldest. A groundsman who had worked

45
00:04:06,727 --> 00:04:11,713
there for twenty-two years and whose father
had worked there before him.

46
00:04:12,112 --> 00:04:15,802
None of those people had ever lived. All
of them were mourned.

47
00:04:16,202 --> 00:04:21,877
He stood in that cemetery for an hour, which is the
longest he is recorded as standing

48
00:04:21,897 --> 00:04:26,513
anywhere without doing anything, and what he
was doing was arithmetic.

49
00:04:26,913 --> 00:04:30,043
The voice that was not his came up
out of the quiet.

50
00:04:30,562 --> 00:04:32,227
You are upset.

51
00:04:32,627 --> 00:04:39,682
I am calculating. Twenty-four thousand graves is
four generations. Four generations is

52
00:04:39,702 --> 00:04:46,758
a history, and a history is a thing people will die
to defend. The System did not give

53
00:04:46,778 --> 00:04:53,917
me a hundred thousand people. It gave me a hundred
thousand people with *grandparents*.

54
00:04:54,317 --> 00:04:56,455
It has given you a nation.

55
00:04:56,855 --> 00:05:00,545
It has given me a *town*. Do
not inflate it.

56
00:05:00,944 --> 00:05:09,287
I built my rallies on less. A crowd that believes it
is ancient will follow you anywhere.

57
00:05:09,307 --> 00:05:16,523
A crowd that knows it was made yesterday will
not follow you across a street.

58
00:05:16,923 --> 00:05:24,171
Wesker looked at a headstone from 1954 with a bunch
of supermarket chrysanthemums on it,

59
00:05:24,191 --> 00:05:25,493
eleven days old.

60
00:05:26,012 --> 00:05:30,983
Then it is fortunate that nobody will ever be
able to tell the difference.

61
00:05:31,382 --> 00:05:33,382
On Monday the city went to work.

62
00:05:33,783 --> 00:05:40,040
The Raccoon City Ledger published an editorial about
the school levy. A traffic camera

63
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:46,464
on Ennerdale Avenue ticketed forty-one drivers. A
woman named Priya Chandrasekhar opened

64
00:05:46,484 --> 00:05:52,742
a bakery that she had opened in 2019, and her
regulars came in, and she knew what they

65
00:05:52,762 --> 00:05:53,253
wanted.

66
00:05:53,653 --> 00:05:59,873
And Albert Wesker drove down from the estate at seven
in the morning in a black car, alone,

67
00:05:59,893 --> 00:06:05,838
and every traffic light on Mill Street turned green
in front of him, and he understood,

68
00:06:05,858 --> 00:06:10,913
four lights in, that this was not a
coincidence and was not going to stop.

69
00:06:11,432 --> 00:06:13,283
I did not ask for that.

70
00:06:13,682 --> 00:06:14,432
No.

71
00:06:14,833 --> 00:06:20,643
Turn it off. Permanently. I do not want a
city that I cannot be late in.

72
00:06:21,043 --> 00:06:22,193
(flat) Off.

73
00:06:22,593 --> 00:06:28,029
The narrator asks you to notice that he meant it, and
to notice why. It was not modesty.

74
00:06:28,049 --> 00:06:33,361
He had understood something in the cemetery that it
would take his enemies eleven more

75
00:06:33,381 --> 00:06:38,817
years to understand: that the only thing that would
ever be able to hurt him was a world

76
00:06:38,837 --> 00:06:43,963
that stopped behaving normally around him, because
then people would start to look.

77
00:06:44,362 --> 00:06:49,745
He met the Mayor and the Chief of Police at ten
o'clock, in an office with a photograph

78
00:06:49,765 --> 00:06:55,085
of a previous mayor on the wall, and the meeting
told him everything he needed to know

79
00:06:55,105 --> 00:06:57,073
about the machine he had bought.

80
00:06:57,473 --> 00:07:03,249
Mayor Elaine Crozier was Absolute. She was
sixty-one, she had served three terms, she

81
00:07:03,269 --> 00:07:09,114
had a husband and a bad knee and a pending lawsuit
about a sewer easement, and when he

82
00:07:09,134 --> 00:07:15,048
asked her what the city needed most she gave him a
list of six items, in order, costed,

83
00:07:15,068 --> 00:07:16,003
and then said:

84
00:07:16,523 --> 00:07:19,084
And whatever you need from us,
Director, you have.

85
00:07:19,485 --> 00:07:26,194
Chief of Police Raymond Okonkwo was not. Integrated —
real, in the way Ray Dorsey in Newark

86
00:07:26,214 --> 00:07:31,075
was real — with nineteen years of memories
and a daughter at Pitt.

87
00:07:31,595 --> 00:07:34,325
With respect, ma'am, that's not
how I'd put it.

88
00:07:34,725 --> 00:07:36,375
The room got very quiet.

89
00:07:36,894 --> 00:07:41,489
Director Wesker, I've got two hundred and eighty
officers and a city that's ninety per

90
00:07:41,509 --> 00:07:46,264
cent employed by one company. That's not unusual in
this state; I grew up in a mill town.

91
00:07:46,284 --> 00:07:51,039
What is unusual is that I can't find the company's
name on a single permit in my building

92
00:07:51,059 --> 00:07:55,868
before last Tuesday, and that when I went looking,
every clerk I asked told me a different

93
00:07:55,888 --> 00:08:00,589
year with total confidence. So I'll ask you plainly,
because I'd rather ask you than the

94
00:08:00,609 --> 00:08:01,984
FBI. What am I looking at?

95
00:08:02,384 --> 00:08:07,375
Wesker turned his head and considered the Chief
of Police for a long moment.

96
00:08:07,774 --> 00:08:13,743
And the narrator would like to record, for the
benefit of everything that happens in the

97
00:08:13,763 --> 00:08:18,915
next sixty years, that what Albert Wesker felt
at that moment was not anger.

98
00:08:19,314 --> 00:08:20,395
It was *delight*.

99
00:08:20,915 --> 00:08:23,225
What is your budget, Chief?

100
00:08:23,625 --> 00:08:25,015
Forty-one million.

101
00:08:25,415 --> 00:08:31,880
It is now ninety. You will report to the Mayor, as you
always have, and you will not report

102
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:38,295
to me, ever, on anything. If one of my people breaks a
law in this valley, you will arrest

103
00:08:38,315 --> 00:08:44,424
them, and I will not call you about it, and if I do
call you about it you may hang up.

104
00:08:44,825 --> 00:08:46,975
That's not an answer to my question.

105
00:08:47,375 --> 00:08:53,937
No. It is an answer to the question underneath it,
which is whether you are going to be

106
00:08:53,957 --> 00:09:00,897
allowed to do your job. You are. You are also going to
spend the next nineteen years finding

107
00:09:00,917 --> 00:09:07,555
nothing, because there is nothing to find, and one
day you will decide that you imagined

108
00:09:07,575 --> 00:09:14,213
the clerks. I would very much prefer that you not
decide that. I would like at least one

109
00:09:14,233 --> 00:09:17,845
honest man in this valley to keep
his eyes open.

110
00:09:18,245 --> 00:09:24,581
The narrator notes that Chief Raymond Okonkwo kept his
eyes open for the rest of his life,

111
00:09:24,601 --> 00:09:31,149
that he never found anything, and that in 2041 he stood
in a stadium with thirty-one thousand

112
00:09:31,169 --> 00:09:32,915
other people and chanted.

113
00:09:33,315 --> 00:09:38,565
A stringer for a Pittsburgh paper came out that week
to write four hundred words about

114
00:09:38,585 --> 00:09:41,875
the company town that everybody had
suddenly heard of.

115
00:09:42,274 --> 00:09:48,057
He interviewed a woman named Margaret Ellsworth on
her porch. She was seventy-eight. She

116
00:09:48,077 --> 00:09:53,860
told him she had lived in Raccoon City since 1961,
that it had been a mill town and then

117
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:59,464
a nothing town and now it was a company town, and
that she had buried a husband here.

118
00:09:59,985 --> 00:10:02,535
And where did you live before 1961, ma'am?

119
00:10:02,934 --> 00:10:05,004
The old woman opened her mouth.

120
00:10:05,899 --> 00:10:11,262
She sat on her porch on a September evening in a
rocking chair her father had made, and

121
00:10:11,282 --> 00:10:16,769
she could not name the town she had been a child in.
She could see it. She could describe

122
00:10:16,789 --> 00:10:20,110
the colour of the kitchen. She could
not say the name.

123
00:10:20,629 --> 00:10:23,620
Well, isn't that something. It's gone
clean out of my head.

124
00:10:24,019 --> 00:10:30,143
She laughed. The stringer laughed too, and wrote
*charming, sharp as a tack, memory going

125
00:10:30,163 --> 00:10:36,356
a bit*, and filed his four hundred words, and that was
the only time in the entire history

126
00:10:36,376 --> 00:10:41,049
of Raccoon City that anybody asked a
citizen that question in print.

127
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:50,054
Territory acquired. Raccoon City, Pennsylvania.
Population one hundred thousand four hundred

128
00:10:50,074 --> 00:10:57,329
and twelve. Civic apparatus complete. Sentiment:
seventy-one per cent favourable. Two

129
00:10:57,349 --> 00:11:04,690
hundred and fifty thousand Points, recurring weekly,
scaled to sentiment. Umbrella now

130
00:11:04,710 --> 00:11:08,371
governs. Govern well; the
ledger is honest.

131
00:11:08,961 --> 00:11:14,318
He climbed to the top row of an empty stadium at
ten o'clock at night and looked down

132
00:11:14,338 --> 00:11:17,165
the valley at a hundred
thousand lit windows.

133
00:11:17,565 --> 00:11:23,275
Two years earlier he had been a man in a chair with a
basement door that did not go anywhere.

134
00:11:23,674 --> 00:11:25,865
The other half of him had an opinion.

135
00:11:26,384 --> 00:11:28,384
Can they be made to chant?

136
00:11:28,784 --> 00:11:34,727
And for once Wesker did not answer immediately. He
stood with his hands behind his back

137
00:11:34,747 --> 00:11:37,263
and looked at the lights for a while.

138
00:11:37,783 --> 00:11:38,673
Yes.

139
00:11:39,072 --> 00:11:39,736
When?

140
00:11:40,138 --> 00:11:46,682
Not for thirteen years. Not until every one of them
owes me something they cannot repay,

141
00:11:46,702 --> 00:11:53,396
and not until the word *Umbrella* means the thing that
keeps their children alive. A crowd

142
00:11:53,416 --> 00:11:58,768
that chants too early is a mob, and a mob is
a thing other men can rent.

143
00:11:59,168 --> 00:12:00,175
And then?

144
00:12:00,575 --> 00:12:06,691
And then I will put ninety thousand of them in this
bowl, and you will tell me where to

145
00:12:06,711 --> 00:12:07,115
stand.
